Fix for clingox UPSTREAM-ISSUES U47: the reference count of an AST node can wrap
around to zero from the C API, freeing a node that handles still point to.

AST::refCount_ is a 32-bit unsigned and AST::incRef is an unchecked increment.
clingo_ast_acquire, the getters clingo_ast_attribute_get_ast,
_get_optional_ast and _get_ast_at, and the SAST copies made when a child is
stored in a parent all go through it. A caller that acquires a node 2^32 times
(leaking every handle) brings the count back to zero; the next release then
deletes the node while other handles are alive, a use after free. Like
std::rc::Rc, the count now aborts the process instead of wrapping. Widening the
counter alone would not help: the limit would only move. Against clingo v5.8.2;
suitable for upstream.

diff --git a/libclingo/src/astv2.cc b/libclingo/src/astv2.cc
--- a/libclingo/src/astv2.cc
+++ b/libclingo/src/astv2.cc
@@ -23,6 +23,8 @@
 // }}}
 
 #include <clingo/astv2.hh>
+#include <cstdlib>
+#include <limits>
 
 namespace Gringo {
 namespace Input {
@@ -235,7 +237,13 @@
     return true;
 }
 
-void AST::incRef() { ++refCount_; }
+void AST::incRef() {
+    if (refCount_ == std::numeric_limits<unsigned>::max()) {
+        // A wrapped count frees the node while handles still point to it.
+        std::abort();
+    }
+    ++refCount_;
+}
 
 void AST::decRef() {
     assert(refCount_ > 0);
